    {
      "id": "iSadNiakMc",
      "source": "openreview",
      "title": "Reset or Reuse? Understanding How Optimizer Memory Shapes Continual Mixture-of-Experts Learning",
      "summary": "Continual fine-tuning of mixture-of-experts (MoE) models, a paradigm that sequentially adapts a pre-trained MoE model to a stream of tasks, has become a popular method in lifelong model deployment because it acquires new capabilities without retraining from scratch.\nHowever, standard sequential fine-tuning carries optimizer state across task boundaries and updates the full parameter space, so cross-task interference from inherited optimization history and from heterogeneous routed experts and always-active modules is neither measured nor controlled, while existing methods instead estimate parameter importance at substantial storage cost.\nTo address this, we investigate the plasticity--forgetting dynamics of continual MoE fine-tuning through boundary-state interventions and role-structured updates, finding that interference degrades both what a new task acquires and what earlier tasks retain, which motivates separating what to discard from what to keep.\nAccordingly, we propose MoRE, a structured optimizer-update scheme for \\emph{moment reset and reuse} that clears the optimizer's fast state at task boundaries, reuses accumulated moment history to adapt the effective learning rate, and allocates subsequent updates across granularities, from role-level gates to per-coordinate modulation.\nOn a six-task sequence from the TRACE continual learning benchmark, MoRE averages $49.53$ on OLMoE-Base, versus $45.63$ for sequential fine-tuning (SeqFT) and $48.39$ for the parameter-regularization baseline EWC;\non Qwen, it reaches $54.90$, versus $51.98$ for SeqFT and $54.60$ for EWC;\nit requires no Fisher estimation pass, and reduces storage for retained coordinate history by $3\\times$ relative to EWC's Fisher-plus-anchor state at equal coverage.",
